  <a href="#section-at-files" id="section-at-files" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>@-files</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      If you run into a character limit in your console, you can also use "argument files" or "@-files", which is
      compatible with <a href="https://picocli.info/#AtFiles">Picocli's argument-file format</a>. The UTF-8 file can contain everything that follows
      <code class="language-text inline">sjm</code>, including the required subcommand and a daemon selector:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">
      # file /home/foo/args
      send
      --email:withSubject "My Subject"
      --mailer:withSMTPServer host=...
      --other arguments
    </code></pre>
    <p class="wide">The command <code class="language-text inline">sjm @/home/foo/args</code> then is expanded to:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">
      sjm send --email:withSubject "My Subject" --mailer:withSMTPServer host=... --other arguments
    </code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Double or single quotes preserve spaces, <code class="inline language-text">#</code> starts a comment outside quotes, and argument files may include
      other argument files within the documented depth and size limits. Relative names resolve from the directory where the client command was invoked.
      Prefix a literal value that starts with <code class="inline language-text">@</code> with a second at sign, for example
      <code class="inline language-text">@@example</code>. The existing <code class="inline language-text">picocli.useSimplifiedAtFiles=true</code>
      mode remains available when every non-comment line should be one argument.
    </p>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-config-properties" id="section-config-properties" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Configuring properties</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      With the provided scripts, anything added to the <code class="inline language-text">etc</code> folder is visible on the classpath. Put
      <code class="inline language-text">simplejavamail.properties</code> there to use it for a CLI command. The command resolves one immutable
      configuration snapshot and uses it for both its email and Mailer builders. Environment variables override the file, and JVM system properties
      override environment variables. A daemon captures these startup sources once; restart that daemon after changing them. Explicit options on each
      command still participate in selecting its reusable Mailer.
    </p>
	  <img data-src="../../assets/sjm etc.png"
	       data-src-webp="../../assets/sjm etc.png.webp"
		   alt="Folder where to put Simple Java Mail properties and logging config for use in CLI">
  </section>


  <a href="#section-config-logging" id="section-config-logging" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Configuring logging</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Log4j2 configuration is supported and you can edit the provided <code class="inline language-text">log4j2.xml</code> in the
      <code class="inline language-text">etc</code> folder. The daemon uses a separate bounded rolling log that omits raw command values, messages, and credentials.
    </p>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-invoking-cli-manually" id="section-invoking-cli-manually" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Invoking CLI manually</h2>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      If you wish to directly invoke the CLI from the jar or class files, CLI support for Java libraries works
      like any other Java static void main invocation on Java 17 or newer:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">java -cp all;the;jars.jar org.simplejavamail.cli.SimpleJavaMail --help</code></pre>
    <p class="wide">
      Simple Java Mail's own main jar should be on the classpath of course, as well as all non-optional dependencies,
      the optional cli-module dependency and <em>its</em> dependencies.
    </p>
    Add <code class="inline language-text">simplejavamail.properties</code> to this classpath to use the same conventional file, environment, and system-property
    resolution as the provided scripts.
  </section>


  <a href="#section-or-by-maven" id="section-or-by-maven" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Or by Maven</h3>

  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Alternatively, you can also let Maven deal with the dependencies, by invoking the maven-exec plugin.
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-text">mvn exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="org.simplejavamail.cli.SimpleJavaMail" -Dexec.args="--help"</code></pre>
    Or by configuring it in your POM:
    <pre><code class="small language-xml">
&lt;build&gt;
  &lt;plugins&gt;
    &lt;plugin&gt;
      &lt;groupId&gt;org.codehaus.mojo&lt;/groupId&gt;
      &lt;artifactId&gt;exec-maven-plugin&lt;/artifactId&gt;
      &lt;version&gt;3.6.3&lt;/version&gt;
      &lt;executions&gt;
        &lt;execution&gt;
          &lt;goals&gt;&lt;goal&gt;java&lt;/goal&gt;&lt;/goals&gt;
        &lt;/execution&gt;
      &lt;/executions&gt;
      &lt;configuration&gt;
        &lt;mainClass&gt;org.simplejavamail.cli.SimpleJavaMail&lt;/mainClass&gt;
        &lt;arguments&gt;
          &lt;argument&gt;--help&lt;/argument&gt;
        &lt;/arguments&gt;
      &lt;/configuration&gt;
    &lt;/plugin&gt;
  &lt;/plugins&gt;
&lt;/build&gt;
    </code></pre>
    <pre><code class="language-text">mvn exec:java</code></pre>
  </section>
